HMMT Feb 2026 -- provenance of everything that came from somewhere else
=======================================================================

Recorded 2026-09-02. Nothing in this directory is a copy of the benchmark. The
33 problems and their answers are fetched at run time from a pinned dataset
revision and are never vendored, because the set is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0
(`dataset.py` module docstring; the README's "Running HMMT Feb 2026" section
repeats the claim). What is reproduced here is the reference harness's grader
semantics and its judge prompt, both of which are the instrument: a grader that
disagreed with the saved reference runs would make those records useless as an
audit and would silently redefine the benchmark (`grader.py` module docstring).

Every fact below is cited to the file it was read from. Where a number came
from a run rather than from a constant, the run is named. The `capability-evals`
runs of the internal `jevals` reference harness live in a private steering-tools
workspace; their records are available to maintainers and are not shipped here,
which is how `hmmt.py`, `grader.py` and `judge.py` all describe them.


1. The benchmark
----------------

Competition The Harvard-MIT Mathematics Tournament, February 2026, as released
            by MathArena (https://matharena.ai,
            https://github.com/eth-sri/matharena). (`hmmt.py` module docstring)

Size        33 problems with one final answer each: the 30 individual-round
            problems plus the 3 extras MathArena scores with them. Asserted at
            load time, because a silently short dataset scores a different
            benchmark under the same name. (`dataset.HMMT_FEB_2026_PROBLEMS`)

Answers     17 of the 33 are non-integer LaTeX -- `\sqrt{69}`, `3+\sqrt{11}`,
            `74^\circ`. That is why the two AIME scorers already published for
            Inspect cannot be reused: both assume an integer answer.
            (`hmmt.py` module docstring; the README repeats the count)

Protocol    avg@4, which is MathArena's protocol for this competition.
            `Epochs(4, "mean")` reduces the four attempts at a problem to that
            problem's success rate before any metric sees them, which is what
            makes the headline the mean over problems rather than over
            generations. (`hmmt.DEFAULT_EPOCHS`)


