3. The prompt
-------------

`REASONING_PROMPT_SUFFIX` is the default instruction appended to each problem:

  "\n\nPlease reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{}."

  sha256  e4af2c73eebe21e7b31b929aad972d553a3143da0cd5b76cd51e42468a32286d

It is kept exactly as it is rather than tidied, because it is the wording behind
the reference runs, recorded in their run configs under that hash, and their
unsteered avg@4 of 0.8409 sits next to the 84.3 MathArena publishes for the same
model on this competition. The prompt is one of three things that number depends
on; the other two are the serving stack and `REFERENCE_SAMPLING`.
(`hmmt.REASONING_PROMPT_SUFFIX`; the hash is pinned as
`scripts/validate_hmmt.py: REASONING_SUFFIX_SHA256` and checked by
`--check grader`)

`MATHARENA_PROMPT_SUFFIX` carries MathArena's own instruction for this
competition, verbatim from `configs/competitions/hmmt/hmmt_feb_2026.yaml` in
eth-sri/matharena. One deviation, deliberate: MathArena puts the instruction
before the problem (`runner.py` appends `"\n\n{problem}"` to the instruction
when the template has no `{problem}` placeholder), while this eval appends it.
The position is documented rather than parameterised, so both constants drop
into the same `prompt_suffix` slot, and `prompt_suffix_sha256` in the task
metadata records which one a run used. (`hmmt.MATHARENA_PROMPT_SUFFIX`)


4. The grader, and what was changed in porting it
-------------------------------------------------

`grader.py` is a port of `jevals/grading.py` from the internal reference
harness, which produced the runs this eval is cross-validated against. Its
rules, in order (`grader.py` module docstring):

  1. Grade only the final channel. A `\boxed{}` written while the model is
     still thinking is a draft, not an answer. (`grader.strip_thinking`)
  2. Take the last `\boxed{...}`, with the closing brace found by counting
     rather than by regex, because `\boxed{\frac{1}{2}}` nests.
     (`grader.extract_boxed`)
  3. Normalise display conventions that carry no mathematical content --
     `\dfrac` vs `\frac`, `\left`/`\right`, thin spaces, `1{,}234`. Deliberately
     conservative: anything that could change the value is left to math-verify.
     (`grader.normalize`)
  4. Compare cheaply first and symbolically last -- exact string, then integer,
     then math-verify -- and record which rung decided, so the results table
     says how much machinery the verdicts needed.
     (`grader.GRADING_METHODS` = `("exact", "int", "math-verify", "none")`;
     `grading_method_share` emits the full fixed key set even for a run in which
     some rung never fires, because Inspect needs the key set to be constant)

Three outcomes are kept apart and never folded together: a wrong answer, a parse
failure (no `\boxed{}` at all, labelled `none`), and `verifier_failed` (a
symbolic comparison that could not be made). The headline accuracy still scores
a parse failure zero, which is how the published numbers treat a missing answer,
but half this dataset's answers are decided by math-verify, so a broken verifier
would otherwise look exactly like a model that lost half its mathematics.
(`grader.py` module docstring)

Two deliberate departures from the harness's function, neither of which changes
a single verdict on the 396 saved reference records:

  a. `strip_thinking` also refuses an unterminated thinking block. Take
     everything after the last `</think>`, then drop everything from an unclosed
     `<think>` onwards, in that order. The order matters: a model that answers,
     reconsiders and is cut off mid-revision produces a closed pair followed by
     an unclosed one, and testing for the closer first would hand the draft back
     as the final answer. The reference runs were produced under the
     separated-reasoning representation -- 0 of the 396 saved records contain
     either tag -- so the addition cannot change any verdict it is compared
     against. (`grader.strip_thinking`)

  b. `gold_in_content` refuses a lenient match that sits inside a longer number.
     `normalize` deletes all whitespace, so the gold `10` occurs inside `110`,
     `2010` and `10.5`, and nine of the 33 answers are three characters or
     fewer. Measured over the 396 reference records, the rate at which a record
     wins lenient credit for a gold belonging to a different problem is 2.38%
     with a bare substring test and 1.50% with the boundary rule, and the rule
     changes none of the reference verdicts -- 0, 2 and 7 lenient hits across
     the three arms, the same records, which is what keeps this comparable with
     their `metrics.json`. The rule was verified against the harness's manual
     review of the -2 arm: exactly 7 hits among its 43 non-correct records and 0
     among the unsteered arm's. It is still not clean, and `lenient` is
     documented as an upper bound rather than an accuracy.
     (`grader.gold_in_content`)


5. math-verify, and the timeout that had to be re-implemented
-------------------------------------------------------------

Dependency  `math-verify>=0.9,<1`, a direct dependency rather than an optional
            one, and its ImportError is deliberately not swallowed: without it
            every symbolic answer would score zero and the run would read as a
            capability drop rather than as a broken environment. Capped below
            1.0 because equivalence semantics are the eval's definition of
            correct, and a major version is where a library is entitled to
            change them. (`pyproject.toml`, with that reasoning written beside
            the pin; the version validated against is 0.9.0)

math-verify implements its own timeouts with `signal.alarm`, which raises
`ValueError` outside the main thread. `parse(..., parsing_timeout=5)` therefore
turns every symbolic comparison into an exception the moment the grader runs on
a worker thread or an event loop's pool -- which is where Inspect runs it. So
its timeouts are disabled at the call site (`parsing_timeout=None`,
`timeout_seconds=None`) and the budget is imposed by a daemon worker thread
instead, where it works on any thread. (`grader._run_bounded`,
`grader._mathverify_equal`)

  VERIFIER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS = 30.0

Generous on purpose. math-verify's own default is 5 seconds and that is the
budget the reference runs graded under, but a sympy comparison that is merely
slow must not be recorded as a wrong answer, and 30 seconds is far beyond
anything the reference records need -- the whole 396-record regrade finishes in
seconds. A comparison that exceeds it is reported as `verifier_failed`, never as
incorrect. A thread that overruns cannot be killed in Python, so it is left
running as a daemon and the process exits without waiting for it; that cost is
bounded by one abandoned thread per undecidable expression, and
`verifier_failed` counts them. (`grader.VERIFIER_TIMEOUT_SECONDS`,
`grader._run_bounded`)


6. The optional equivalence judge
---------------------------------

Off by default and a diagnostic view only: the mechanical grader is the eval's
definition of correct, and this scorer exists to say how much of a run's failure
is the model getting the mathematics wrong and how much is it declining to put
the answer in a box. (`judge.py` module docstring)

The prompt is taken from `jevals/judge.py` in the reference harness and kept
word for word, including the clause refusing a rounded decimal, which is what
stops the judge from handing back the credit `gold_in_content` deliberately
withholds.

  sha256  02574357a53cb1615beb8899b0793fe0ef4165b3e72163b153cb4bac70a5fa37

Only the source formatting differs: the one long paragraph is split across
adjacent string literals to stay inside the line limit, and `--check grader`
asserts the assembled string still hashes to that value.
(`judge.JUDGE_PROMPT`, `judge.JUDGE_PROMPT_SHA256`)

Three deviations from the harness's implementation, each argued where it occurs:
the reply and the problem are sent in full rather than clipped, the verdict is
requested as structured output with the free-text fallbacks kept underneath, and
the judge's own reasoning is requested and stored. Every verdict carries
`judge_reasoning` in its score metadata -- the summarised reasoning in full, or
`None` when the provider returned none -- under the house rule that a model
call's reasoning is captured or its absence is recorded. (`judge.py` module
docstring)

`DEFAULT_JUDGE_MAX_TOKENS = 4096`, deliberately below Inspect's 8192-token
auto-streaming threshold: Anthropic's SDK dropped `temperature` from
`messages.stream()`, so a streamed judge call raises TypeError and every sample
silently scores an error instead of a verdict.
(`judge.DEFAULT_JUDGE_MAX_TOKENS`)


7. The sampling policy behind every reference number
----------------------------------------------------

  REFERENCE_SAMPLING = {"temperature": 1.0, "top_p": 0.95, "top_k": 20}

Recorded from `results/full/hmmt__nosteer/config.json` in the reference
harness's `capability-evals` runs: Qwen's published thinking-mode settings for
`Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B`. Every reference number in this package is a measurement of
this policy, and comparing against them under a different one compares two
things. (`hmmt.REFERENCE_SAMPLING`)

The task does not impose it. A default temperature is a property of the model
under test, not of the benchmark, and hard-coding Qwen's settings into a
provider-agnostic eval would repeat the mistake upstream ctfish makes (see
`inspect_evals/ctfish/config.py`). It is offered instead, as
`hmmt_feb_2026(generate_config=reference_generate_config())`.

Why a helper rather than a plain `GenerateConfig`: `top_k` is a real
`GenerateConfig` field and is never read on Inspect's OpenAI-compatible request
path, which the `steered` provider and Inspect's own `vllm` provider both
inherit. Setting it looks right in the eval log and changes nothing about the
request, so it has to travel in `extra_body`, which is forwarded verbatim and
which `SteeredAPI.completion_params` merges into rather than replaces, so the
steering arguments and these coexist. (`hmmt.reference_generate_config`)

Four epochs only mean four attempts if the provider samples. Against a server
whose default is greedy decoding all four are the same generation: `correct`
quietly becomes pass@1 at four times the cost, and the item bootstrap narrows
because the attempts agree rather than because the model is sure. Nothing in the
metric table would look wrong. (`hmmt.DEFAULT_EPOCHS`)

The output budget is a measurement too:

  DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS = 81920

At this budget the reference unsteered run still truncated 11 of 132
generations, with a mean completion of 39,672 tokens; the -2 steering arm
truncated 22. Lowering it would measure the budget rather than the mathematics,
and `hmmt_diagnostics` reports `truncated_generations` precisely so that a run
where it is the budget cannot be read as a capability result. Those counts are
measurements under `REFERENCE_SAMPLING`, not constants: how long a thinking
model thinks is a function of its sampling policy. (`hmmt.DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS`)

Reference numbers, for reading a new run against (README, "Running HMMT Feb
2026", and `hmmt.REASONING_PROMPT_SUFFIX`):

  0.841   unsteered avg@4, `correct`, `Qwen/Qwen3.6-27B`
  0.876   the same run's `hmmt_untruncated_scorer` accuracy
  0.917   the harness's own figure for that second number, which differs because
          it averages over records rather than over problems; its published
          interval is a bootstrap over problems, so it is this eval's estimator
          that interval brackets, not its point estimate
  0.765   the +2 steering arm
  0.674   the -2 steering arm
  84.3    what MathArena publishes for this model on this competition

A healthy unsteered run on this model lands between 0.75 and 0.92. The
agreement with MathArena is why the default prompt suffix is kept exactly as it
is, but the comparison stays directional: the sampling policy, the serving stack
and the prompt are this repository's, and MathArena's own instruction is shorter
and sits before the problem rather than after it.


8. The regrade parity, which is the strongest free evidence here
----------------------------------------------------------------

`scripts/validate_hmmt.py --check regrade` re-grades the completions of a saved
reference run with this eval's grader and diffs five things against that run's
own, all five of which can fail the check: the verdicts, the extracted strings,
the lenient-hit count, the record-level accuracy and the item bootstrap. The
gate is `MIN_REGRADE_AGREEMENT_RATIO = 130 / 132` -- a ratio rather than a
count, because the plan's ">=130/132" is a proportion and only the three known
arms happen to hold 132 records each: as an absolute threshold it would fail a
perfect regrade of a 33-record smoke and pass a larger directory on a third of
the verdicts agreeing. Both directions were reproduced before it was changed to
a ratio. (`scripts/validate_hmmt.py: MIN_REGRADE_AGREEMENT_RATIO` and
`check_regrade`; the result below is recorded in commit f280b71, 2026-08-31,
"Add HMMT Feb 2026 as an inspect_ai eval")

Result across the three reference arms (unsteered, +2, -2):

  396/396   verdicts identical
  396/396   extracted strings identical
  0         verifier failures

That is a controlled comparison on the grader rather than an assertion about it:
the same completions, the same golds, two independent implementations. It is
also why the two deviations in section 4 can be stated as changing no verdict --
they were run against these records, not reasoned about.

`--check regrade` needs the records and is therefore not part of `--check all`,
which runs `dataset`, `grader` and `mockllm`. None of those three needs an API
key; `dataset` needs Hugging Face. (`scripts/validate_hmmt.py` module docstring)


9. The 600-second timeout trap
------------------------------

This is a fact about serving rather than about the benchmark, and it is recorded
here because HMMT is the eval where it bites: `DEFAULT_MAX_TOKENS` is 81,920 and
a reasoning model on a self-hosted server can spend well over ten minutes on one
problem.

Inspect 0.3.259's OpenAI-compatible providers -- including this repository's
`steered/` provider, which subclasses `OpenAICompatibleAPI` -- build their HTTP
client from the OpenAI SDK's defaults when no `client_timeout` is given, and
those are `Timeout(connect=5.0, read=600, write=600, pool=600)`
(`_providers/openai_compatible.py`, `_create_http_client` and `_create_client`).
Past 600 seconds the request raises `APITimeoutError`, the retry starts the same
generation from scratch, and it times out again: the run neither finishes nor
fails, it loops and keeps the GPU busy.

`GenerateConfig(timeout=...)` does not fix it. That value bounds the retry loop
through tenacity's `stop_after_delay` and never reaches the HTTP client. The
model argument is the only thing that moves the read timeout, as
`-M client_timeout=7200` or `get_model(..., client_timeout=7200.0)`.
(`hmmt.py` module docstring)

`scripts/run_hmmt.py` sets it -- `CLIENT_TIMEOUT = 7200.0`, carried in
`MODEL_ARGS` -- and drops it for `anthropic/*`, whose provider does not take the
argument and does not carry the 600-second default either.
(`scripts/run_hmmt.py`, `CLIENT_TIMEOUT`, `MODEL_ARGS` and the filter beneath
them)


10. What this package does not vendor, and why that is checkable
----------------------------------------------------------------

No problem text, no answer, no dataset file. The set is CC BY-NC-SA 4.0 and a
runtime pin plus a digest is enough to make a run reproducible
(`dataset.py` module docstring). The two things that would be tempting to copy
are pinned by hash instead: the answer key by `ANSWER_KEY_SHA256`, and the
prompt and judge prompt by `REASONING_SUFFIX_SHA256` and `JUDGE_PROMPT_SHA256`.
All three are asserted by `scripts/validate_hmmt.py`, so a drift in any of them
fails a free, offline-or-Hugging-Face-only check rather than surfacing as a
number nobody can explain.

This file ships in the wheel for the same reason the other evals' do: an
installed copy should still be able to answer "where did this number come from"
without the repository. (`pyproject.toml`, `[tool.setuptools.package-data]`)
